this video is brought to you by talk in  arabic the only place where you can  learn  any of the major dialects of spoken  arabic if you want to learn arabic the  way it's really spoken  click the link in the description  indonesian and malay  which are basically two varieties of the  same language are members of the  austronesian language family  but due to contact with other cultures  through trade religion and colonialism  they've been influenced to a great  extent by other languages  their major influences include dutch and  english depending on  who colonized the area sanskrit as well  as persian and  arabic today there's a significant  number of arabic loanwords in indonesian  and malay  not only formal literary and religious  vocabulary  but lots of common words used in  everyday conversation also come from  arabic  if you stick around until the end of the  video i'll tell you exactly how many  arabic loanwords there  are the middle east and north africa are  far from southeast asia  so how did arabic influence malay  nusantara  which includes present-day malaysia  indonesia brunei and singapore has been  a hub of international trade since as  early as the 4th century  most notably the strait of malacca  contact with muslim arab traders goes  back as far as the 7th century  and they were later followed by muslim  missionaries some of them from the  indian subcontinent  and some of them from the arabian  peninsula islam became widespread by the  14th century  partly through the conversion of local  traders but in large part by converting  and  intermarrying with members of the ruling  classes which caused much of the  community to imitate their leaders and  convert to islam as well  during the malacca sultanate which was  founded in the year 1400  the spread of islam accelerated and  malay became widespread as a lingua  franca of trade  malacca also became a busy international  port city  resulting in more borrowings from other  languages and this is probably when some  of the more conversational arabic words  entered the language  and during this period the influence of  islam and islamic literature  brought more formal arabic vocabulary  into malay let's look first at some of  the religious vocabulary that was  borrowed from arabic the word for god  allah in malay and indonesian this word  is not only used by muslims  it is the general word for god and is  also used by christians  though in malaysia there has been some  controversy about that  masjid or in malay masjid the word from  mosque  from arabic masjid iman the word for  faith  from arabic iman ibada or  ibada meaning worship from arabic  in indonesian this is the word for a  head scarf covering a female's hair  head and shoulders in arabic the word  gilbert  refers to a type of long outer cloak  that covers the whole body  haram the word meaning forbidden or  illicit  from arabic haram one interesting  borrowing is the word  kitab from the arabic word kitab in  arabic this refers to any book  but in malay and indonesian it normally  refers to a holy book  including the holy books of other  religions though sometimes it refers to  a different kind of book in a very  literary context  for example a dictionary may sometimes  be referred to as  kitab logat this word logat also comes  from arabic  luga the word for language in malay and  indonesian it can mean an accent or  vernacular  so this phrase means something like book  of vernacular  but the usual word for dictionary is  kamus which is also an arabic loanword  from arabic  kamus there are a lot of simple everyday  words like this that come from arabic  salamat this word means safety or  well-being  it comes from arabic salama meaning  safety  this word is used constantly in malayan  indonesian because it's used for  greetings  such as salamat pagi meaning good  morning  it's also used with the meaning of  congratulations  to ask someone how they are in malay you  ask apocabar  meaning what's the news this word khabar  means  news when referring to personal news  this comes from the arabic word for news  to say sorry in malayan indonesian you  say ma'af  this comes from the arabic word meaning  exemption  so it's like saying please give me an  exemption from punishment or  please let me off the hook the malay and  indonesian word for time  like time in a general sense is waktu  this comes from the arabic word for time  this word means moment or time in the  sense of  at the time of it comes from arabic  meaning hour or time and nowadays watch  or clock too  a little side note here notice that this  word saat  as well as salamat end with the t in  malay and indonesia  even though they end in an ah or a sound  in arabic  why is that well it's because those  arabic nouns end in a t  sound when they connect to something  after them like a case ending  or another noun in a genitive noun  phrase like  ahmed's watch for many words the  connecting form was borrowed rather than  the base form  kurusi the word for chair this comes  from arabic  the word for paper is karitas this comes  from the arabic word  kritas which means a sheet of paper or  parchment  the word for problem masala this comes  from the arabic word masala  which means problem in the sense of an  issue or a matter  the word for body badan this comes  straight from the arabic word  badan which means body or trunk the word  for gift  hadiya this comes from the arabic word  hadiya  the word meaning poor miskin this comes  from the arabic word  muskin which means poor as in poor guy  like you feel sorry for someone  the malayan indonesian word means  monetarily poor  musim this word means season it comes  from the arabic word for season  mausim the borrowed word is also used  for the names of specific seasons like  mussim panas the term for summer  literally hot season  istirahat this word is a noun meaning  rest or break  or a verb meaning to rest or take a  break it comes from the arabic word  istiraha  malay including indonesian is a language  in which affixes are often added to  roots to create new words  so oftentimes arabic roots are  surrounded by malay affixes  daftara the noun meaning list is daftar  this comes from the arabic word daftar  but the malay word also serves as a root  word to which affixes can be added  mandaftar is a verb meaning to register  or enroll  mandaftarakan is a transitive verb  meaning to register or enroll something  or someone  the word meaning answer jawab it comes  from arabic  jaweb but then an affix is normally  added to the root  mun jawab zaya sudah i already answered  him  the root takes a verbal prefix and yeah  is an  object pronoun suffix meaning him in  this case but it could also mean her  or it there are numerous arabic words  that i hear frequently in indonesian  because they're used as transition words  or as  adverbials to move the conversation  forward or to identify  important information the words take the  suffix  which is generally used to refer back to  something that has previously been  mentioned or is understood  this is like saying i mean or that is to  say  you use it at the beginning of a  sentence when you want to clarify what  you mean  maksud this word means either intention  or meaning  it comes from the arabic word maksud  meaning intention or purpose  subanya this is like saying that's  because or  the reason for that is it's used to  express cause and effect  this word sabab means reason and it  comes from the arabic word for reason  sabab so anya this is like saying  the thing is it's used to tell the  listener that you are about to add some  important information about what has  just been said  this word sowal means problem issue or  question  it comes from the arabic word which  means  question or query um  this means in general um means general  common or public as an adjective or noun  it comes from the arabic noun  which means generality prevalence total  or  public as in the public this means at  first  or in the beginning awal means beginning  and comes from the arabic ordinal number  awal which means first  this means finally or in the end ahir  means end and comes from the arabic word  which means last as an adjective and end  as a noun  these words occur very frequently with  the suffix  but they're also common without it for  example akhir  it's also used as a verb meaning to end  and you hear the noun in all sorts of  contexts like  meaning weekend i listen to the news in  indonesian every morning and there are  certain words  of arabic origin that i hear over and  over  this phrase means natural disaster alam  means nature and comes from an arabic  word meaning world  corban meaning the victim's body in this  phrase both words come from arabic but  their meanings have evolved into  something different  in indonesian janazah means corpse or  dead body but in arabic it means funeral  janeza and in indonesian korban means  victim  but the arabic word quran means a  sacrifice to god  or a sacrificial animal uila  this word means territory or arya it  comes from the arabic word wilaya  which means province or govern it dunya  this means world it comes from the  arabic word dunya  america the united states the second  word here sarika  means union association or league and  comes from the arabic word  jarika which means company though its  original meaning when it was borrowed  was more like  a partnership or association honestly  there are lots more and i could stand  here all day listing them all  so let's move on and look at a few news  headlines and see if we can find  any arabic loanwords populasi badak  sumatra through sturon akibatsuli kawin  program by taboon jadisolusi this  sentence means the population of  sumatran rhinos continues to fall as a  result of difficulty mating  ivf program becomes a solution in this  sentence there is one arabic loanword  akibat akibat means result or  consequence  it comes from meaning consequence  the word kawin means to mate or to get  married  i found some conflicting information  about its etymology with one source  saying it came from the arabic word for  dowry  but it seems that it actually came from  persian  this sentence means the who says it  doesn't agree with countries making  covid vaccinations obligatory  in this sentence there is one arabic  loanword wajib which means required or  obligatory  the arabic word wajib is a noun meaning  obligation or duty  here the indonesian word has a verb  suffix that makes this a transitive verb  with the meaning of  make obligatory wajib khan  this means a number of ways to lose body  weight in two weeks that you can try in  order to support your diet goals  the first word is an arabic loanword  joomla meaning number  amount or total it comes from arabic  zoom le  meaning a series or collection or a  sentence or phrase  the prefix means one and is functioning  here similar to an indefinite article  another word from arabic here is badan  meaning body  which we saw before one word that might  look like an arabic loanword but  isn't is anda it resembles the arabic  second person masculine singular pronoun  enta but anda is not from arabic it was  introduced into modern indonesian and  malay in the 20th century  based on an obscure and archaic malay  word  as you can see there's a significant  amount of arabic vocabulary in  indonesian and malay but nowhere near a  majority of the words  in the camus daewon dictionary of the  malay language 1791 words come from  arabic  comprising 6.25 percent of the entries  some of those are uncommon literary  words though the number of commonly used  words is 1005  comprising 3.5 percent of the entries  but that 3.5 percent is not  insignificant  because it includes quite a few words  that are used constantly  the question of the day for speakers of  malay and indonesian did you know that  the words in the video came from arabic  do you have any other examples of arabic  loanwords and to speakers 